  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "This release makes the graph/step lookup engines the only lookup engines, and makes every engine result report the snap token it was read at. As a result, enabling caching no longer costs a round trip to resolve the latest token. It also fixes queries left running by a finished check failing once the request scope is disposed.\n\n## Breaking changes\n\n* **The V2 lookup engines are the only lookup engines** (#328).  registers them. The recursive engines (, ) and the opt-in methods  and  are removed. Delete those calls; nothing else changes for callers. The engines return the same results; see the v1.6.0 notes for how they run and how they compare.\n* **Engine results carry the snap token they were read at** (#329):\n\n  | Method | 1.6 | 2.0 |\n  |---|---|---|\n  |  |  |  (, ) |\n  |  |  |  (, ) |\n  |  |  |  (, ) |\n  |  |  | , plus a  on the page |\n  |  |  | , plus a  on the result |\n\n  Read ,  or  where you used the value directly. Await each call once, and use  where you need a , for example for . A result's token is null only when the request carried none and the answer read no stored data.\n* **Requests are immutable** (#329). ,  and  are -only; use  to derive one request from another.\n* **Removed public types and members:**\n  * ,  (#328)\n  *  (#329)\n  *  on ,  and  (#330)\n* **Custom engines** (, ,  implementations) should report the token they read at.  caches a result under that token, and does not cache a result without one.\n\nSee Upgradingfrom1.6 in [Using the Engines](https://github.com/valtuutus/valtuutus/blob/main/Using the Engines.md#upgrading-from-16) for the full list with examples.\n\n## New: snap tokens on results\n\nPass a result's  to a later request to read the same snapshot, for example to make a check and a lookup agree. When paging :\n* pass each page's  to the next request to read every page from one snapshot;\n* or leave it unset to read each page at the latest snapshot.\n\n## Fixes\n\n* **Caching no longer spends a round trip resolving the latest token** (#329).\n  * **Before:** when the cached latest token had expired (5-minute lifetime), the caching decorators called  as a separate query before every check or lookup. The engine then always got a token, so it never resolved the token inside its own first round trip.\n  * **Now:** without a known token, the decorator calls the engine tokenless and caches the result under the token the engine reports. It records that token as the latest unless the cache already knows a newer one.\n* **Queries a check leaves running keep working after the request scope is disposed** (#330). A check returns as soon as its answer is known, while queries of branches it no longer needs keep running. Disposing the request scope disposed the reader's concurrency semaphore under them. A leftover query that then needed it threw , or hung if it was already waiting for a slot.\n\n## Performance\n\n and the new result structs, measured against v1.6.0 plus #328 (net10.0 BenchmarkDotNet, median of 3 interleaved rounds):\n\n| Provider | Time | Allocated per call |\n|---|---|---|\n| InMemory | within ±4% everywhere | Check unchanged; SubjectPermission and LookupSubject −72 B; LookupEntity −64 B |\n| Postgres | −13%..+11%, no consistent direction | +72..+113 B |\n| SQL Server | −2%..+7.5%, within this provider's run-to-run spread | +72..+113 B |\n\n* A cache-hit  completes without allocating a task, where  would cost 88 B.\n* A database-backed call carries the larger result through its async chain, about 1% of what it already allocates.\n\n## Pull requests\n\n* #328 chore!: make the V2 lookup engines the only lookup engines\n* #329 feat!: report the snap token every engine result was read at\n* #330 fix(data): keep serving stragglers after the request scope is disposed\n* #319 Build(deps): Bump EnricoMi/publish-unit-test-result-action",
